TITLE:: SDGSampleEditor
summary:: a window on one sample of the sound library: trim, gain, DC, fades, numbers, save back into the bank
categories:: Libraries>SuperDirtGUI
related:: Classes/SuperDirtGUI, Classes/SDGWindow, Classes/SDGSampleEdit, Classes/DirtSoundLibrary

DESCRIPTION::
The waveform, one lane per channel, with the trim markers on the top strip and
the fade handles on the bottom; boxes for the same in milliseconds; a gain knob
with normalize to a ceiling; remove DC; a table of numbers for the file
as it is and for what the save would write; play and stop through an orbit;
reset, revert, save, save as.

code::
~sdg.edit(\bd, 3);                                   // bank, index - what the explorer's edit button does
SDGSampleEditor(~sdg, \bd, 3, 2);                    // the same, previewing through d3
SDGSampleEditor.forPath("~/somewhere/kick.wav", ~sdg);   // a file that is not in the library
::

Nothing is written until save. The file is read into the language
(link::Classes/SDGSampleEdit::), every setting sits on top of it, and the
preview is the library's own Buffer through a small synth at the head of the
orbit's group into its dry bus - so it is heard through that orbit's effects
and fader - with the trim, the gain, the fades and the DC applied on the way.
The orbit is woken first (link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-wakeOrbit::), since
SuperDirt's monitor sleeps after four seconds of silence.

strong::save:: writes over the file, in its own header and sample format, and
keeps the original beside it as code::.bak:: the first time only; then
link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-reloadSample:: reads the new file into the same
Buffer, so Tidal plays it at once under the same name and number.
strong::revert:: copies the code::.bak:: back. strong::save as...:: writes a
new file; into the bank's own folder and it is appended to the bank as its
last index. An integer format is clipped just under full scale on the way out;
a float file keeps its overs.

The numbers are exact, in the language: peak, rms and crest from one pass
over the samples, the DC offset of each channel, integrated loudness and the
loudest 400 ms window K-weighted and gated as ITU-R BS.1770 says - a stretch
shorter than a block is read as one block and says code::(short)::. Loudness
sums channels as the spec does: identical left and right read 3 dB above mono.

Under the header is what the analysis says about the file
(link::Classes/SDGSampleFeatures::, kept by link::Classes/SDGSampleTags::): the
bpm, sure, unsure or by hand; where a beat falls; the key, or the note of a
one-shot; the tags; and the numbers they came from. The window analyses the
whole file when it opens, in the background (link::#-analyse::). With a bpm the
beat grid is drawn over the wave: bars numbered in the accent colour, beats,
and the slicer's division, each when there is room. ÷2 and ×2 halve and double
it, to 1st hit puts a beat on the first attack (link::#-snapGridToFirstHit::),
alt + drag on the wave slides it. A bpm, key or tags set here are the file's
from then on, by hand: the explorer and Tidal see them.

The slicer (link::Classes/SDGSlicer::) cuts the trim on the grid at a division,
or on the attacks at a sensitivity, and shows the cuts on the wave as they move
- in attacks mode with the onset curve they come from. shift + click on the
wave adds a cut, or takes away the one under the pointer. export slices...
(link::#-exportSlicesDialog::) asks where, in the Finder's folder dialog, and
under what name, then writes every slice to a new folder of that name, each file
named after it with the slice's index from 0, and loads it as a bank; the file
itself is not written to.

Keys, when the window has focus: space plays and stops, code::0:: shows the
whole file, code::-:: and code::=:: zoom, code::f:: zooms to the trim, code::g::
shows and hides the grid, left and right slide, cmd-s saves. In the waveform:
cmd + two fingers up and down zooms at the pointer, two fingers on their own
slide, a double click shows the whole file, alt + double click the trim. On Windows and Linux it is ctrl where
it says cmd (link::#*isShortcut::), and the wheel where it says two fingers.

One editor per sample: opening a sample that is already open fronts its
window. The list is link::#*all::.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
ARGUMENT:: sdg
the link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI::.
ARGUMENT:: name
the bank.
ARGUMENT:: index
the sample in it.
ARGUMENT:: orbit
the orbit index the preview plays through.
RETURNS:: the SDGSampleEditor, open - the one already open on that sample if
there is one.

METHOD:: forPath
An editor on a file outside the library; save touches nothing in the library.
ARGUMENT:: path
the file.
ARGUMENT:: sdg
the loader; code::SuperDirtGUI.current:: when nil.
ARGUMENT:: orbit
the orbit index the preview plays through.

METHOD:: all
The open editors.

METHOD:: folderChooser
A function code::{ |start, ok| }:: used in place of the Finder's folder dialog,
answering a folder through code::ok::; nil for the dialog. Checks set it.

METHOD:: chooseFolder
The Finder's folder dialog, or link::#*folderChooser::.
ARGUMENT:: start
the folder it opens at.
ARGUMENT:: ok
called with the folder picked, without a trailing slash, on AppClock; not called on cancel.

METHOD:: windowBounds
Where the last closed editor was; the next one opens there.

METHOD:: isShortcut
Whether a key's modifiers are the shortcut modifier: cmd on macOS, ctrl on
Windows and Linux - which is how QtCollider reports the key there.
ARGUMENT:: mod
the modifiers a keyDownAction is given.

METHOD:: saveKeyName
code::"cmd-s":: on macOS, code::"ctrl-s":: elsewhere: what the hints call the
save shortcut.

METHOD:: previewDef
METHOD:: previewDefName
The preview SynthDef for a sample with that many channels into that many, and
its name, code::sdg_editpreview_<src>_<out>::.


INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: edit
The link::Classes/SDGSampleEdit:: behind the window.

METHOD:: sdg
METHOD:: dirt
The loader and its SuperDirt.

METHOD:: orbitIndex
The orbit the preview plays through; the menu in the window sets it.

METHOD:: play
METHOD:: stop
METHOD:: isPlaying
The preview. It frees itself at the end of the trim.

METHOD:: refresh
Everything on screen from the model.

METHOD:: analyse
The features of the whole file again, in a Routine on AppClock; the grid
takes the file's bpm as the library knows it, or the analysis's.
ARGUMENT:: force
true analyses the file in the library's store again too.

METHOD:: info
What link::Classes/SDGSampleTags#-infoOf:: says about the file.

METHOD:: store
The link::Classes/SDGSampleTags:: of the loader.

METHOD:: setBpm
The grid's bpm, kept for the file by hand; nil takes the grid away.
ARGUMENT:: bpm
a number, or nil.

METHOD:: setKey
ARGUMENT:: str
a key, code::"Am"::, code::"F#"::, code::"C3"::; empty for the analysis's.

METHOD:: setTags
ARGUMENT:: words
the file's tags, all of them.

METHOD:: snapGridToFirstHit
A beat of the grid on the first attack of the file, to the sample
(link::Classes/SDGSampleEdit#-refineOnset::).

METHOD:: slicing
Whether the slicer is on, as its check box.

METHOD:: slicer
The link::Classes/SDGSlicer:: while the slicer is on, else nil.

METHOD:: exportSlices
Every slice into a folder, then a bank, with a hint saying how to play it.
ARGUMENT:: folder
the new folder's path; nil for link::Classes/SDGSlicer#-defaultFolder::, beside the file.
RETURNS:: what link::Classes/SDGSlicer#-export:: answers, or nil.

METHOD:: exportSlicesDialog
The export slices... button: link::#*chooseFolder:: at
link::Classes/SDGSlicer#*exportStart::, then a window asking for the folder's
name, showing the files it will write; return or export writes them through
link::#-exportSlices::, escape cancels.

METHOD:: exportDialog
While that window is open: code::(window:, folder:, export:, preview:)::, where
code::folder:: and code::preview:: are functions answering the place and the
text shown, and code::export.(name):: exports under a name as the button does.

METHOD:: window
METHOD:: isOpen
METHOD:: front
METHOD:: close
The window's state. Closing stops the preview, frees a buffer read for a file
outside the library, and forgets the editor.
